  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 115 PM MST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nWarm and dry weather looks to continue for the forseeable future.\nA weak and moisture starved clipper system will traverse near \nthe area on Tuesday into Wednesday. A few sprinkles or flurries \nwill be possible, but only a small minority of ensemble members \nsupport any precipitation whatsoever for the region. Expect this\nto knock 700-mb temperatures down to the 0 to +2C range, while \npulling the surface trough well east of the Laramie Range. This \nwill allow breezy conditions to spread into the High Plains. \nTemperatures are expected to remain above seasonal averages, but\nwith the cool down we should be a little closer to normal \n(perhaps 5 to 8F above average). While most will be breezy, high\nwinds are fairly unlikely. In-house guidance shows \nprobabilities around 20 to 30% for the typical wind prone areas.\n\nBehind the departing clipper system, ensembles are in excellent \nagreement showing a highly amplified ridge building in over the \nRockies Thursday into the following weekend. This feature will block \nor deflect any incoming storm systems, and push temperatures well \nabove average once again. Since we'll be on the periphery of this \nridge, temperature expectations are not too extreme. 700-mb \ntemperatures around +4 to +6C are currently shown in the NAEFS mean, \nwhich is near the climatological 90th percentile. This isn't quite \nwarm enough to support record highs, but expect temperatures at the \nsurface to range a good 10 to 15F above seasonal norms. The strong \nridge will likely also keep winds fairly light during this\nperiod.",
